{"entries":[{"name":"広田","reading":"ひろた","category":"character","keys":["広田","広田さん","広田先生","髭のある男","髭のある人","男","水蜜桃の先生","青木堂の先生","水蜜桃の男"],"content":"A former high school teacher of 三四郎 and 野々宮, who was previously the unnamed bearded man on the third-class train. He is about forty, with a long, thin face, a thick beard, and a straight nose, and he looks something like a Shinto priest. He shares peaches with 三四郎 on the train and tells him about 子規. He visited 野々宮's sister in hospital, and 三四郎 now sees him drinking tea and smoking alone at 青木堂, wearing a suit. 三四郎 had earlier suspected that this man was the teacher who rode the stubborn horse at the main gate and was laughed at by a craftsman from 喜多床; the passage confirms that the horse incident was indeed Hirota, and 三四郎 concludes that the peach-eating man must be him too, though he admits this is somewhat unreasonable. 与次郎 is with him, and 三四郎 bows to him, neither too formal nor too casual."},{"name":"三四郎","reading":"さんしろう","category":"character","keys":["三四郎","三四郎は"],"content":"The protagonist of the novel, a student from Kumamoto who is travelling by third-class train toward Tokyo. He is still in school, and he wears an old cap with a school insignia. On the train he talks with a bearded man, whom he later learns is 広田, and he feels that man's words have become meaningful since he began attending university lectures. Later he watches two women on a hill by a pond at sunset, and he is fascinated by their colours. He visits 野々宮 and is startled by a woman he calls 池の女 at the end of a long corridor, and he memorises her posture and clothes. He then meets 広田 and 与次郎, and bows to 広田 with his cap in an in-between manner."},{"name":"団扇を持った女","reading":"うちわをもったおんna","category":"character","keys":["団扇を持った女","団扇の女","若いほう","若い女","女","池の女"],"content":"A young woman in a bright, colourful kimono with a white obi patterned with white susuki grass, who stands with a companion on a hill by a pond at sunset. At first she shades her forehead with a fan. Later she puts the fan down and carries a small white flower, which she sniffs while walking. She stops near 三四郎 and asks about a tree, and she looks at him directly for a moment. She drops the white flower in front of him as she leaves, and she has a white rose in her hair. Later, 三四郎 sees a woman he calls 池の女 standing at the bright end of a long corridor at the entrance, and she turns and looks back before they pass each other; he takes in her posture and clothes."},{"name":"野々宮","reading":"ののみや","category":"character","keys":["野々宮","野々宮君"],"content":"A friend of 三四郎 who works in a science field, and who is associated with the light-pressure research. He did not sleep well the previous night because he stayed at a hospital, where his younger sister is a patient and wanted him to stay. He was visited by 広田, his former high school teacher, who came to see the sister, and they talked until late. He says he is dull today and plans to sleep before going to a school in Waseda in the afternoon, when the university is closed. He criticises his sister harshly, calling her a fool, although he does not want to talk about her further."},{"name":"与次郎","reading":"よじろう","category":"character","keys":["与次郎","与次郎の声"],"content":"A student who is a classmate of 三四郎 and who studied at the high school in Kumamoto before coming to Tokyo. He is with 広田 when 三四郎 meets him, and he speaks with unusual precision today. He introduces 三四郎 to 広田 as his classmate who came to Tokyo from the Kumamoto high school. 三四郎 bows more politely to 与次郎 than to 広田."},{"name":"青木堂","reading":"あおきどう","category":"place","keys":["青木堂"],"content":"A teahouse where 広田 sits alone drinking tea and smoking. 三四郎 saw him there earlier, which made 三四郎 remember him more clearly. 三四郎 considers approaching him there, but 広田 keeps facing forward and gives no opening."},{"name":"喜多床","reading":"きたどこ","category":"place","keys":["喜多床","喜多床の職人"],"content":"A place whose craftsman laughed at 三四郎 after he was troubled by a stubborn horse at the main gate of the university. 三四郎 had linked that incident to 広田, and it is now confirmed that the horse incident was indeed 広田."}]}
