{"entries":[{"name":"吾輩","reading":"わがはい","category":"character","keys":["吾輩","猫","吾輩は猫である"],"content":"The first-person narrator, a kitten who has no name yet ('名前はまだ無い'). He was born somewhere dark and damp and cried there, then was carried off by a 書生 and abandoned in a bamboo grove beside a large pond. Hungry and cold, he crept into a nearby house through a gap in a broken bamboo fence, where he was repeatedly thrown out by おさん. He later says he stole something of おさん's called 三馬 and that this was his retaliation, though the passage breaks off before explaining it. He regularly visits the neighbouring cat 三毛 through the same gap in the fence. The 下女 dragged him to the master, who said to let him stay, so he decided to make the house his home and was given the kitchen floor. He lives with the 主人, sitting on his lap or back and sleeping on the rice tub, the kotatsu, or the veranda, but his favourite place is sleeping among the 小供 at night. He admires the neighbouring cat 白君 and observes that the people of the house are selfish and cruel. He was once struck on the bottom with a ruler by the 主人 after the children woke and cried out."},{"name":"書生","reading":"しょせい","category":"character","keys":["書生","書生さん"],"content":"A student-type young man who first picks up the narrator kitten and lifts him onto his palm. The narrator later learns that 書生 are considered the most fearsome kind of human, and that they are said to catch cats and boil them to eat. He is unnamed in the passage. He smokes tobacco, which the narrator finds odd and which makes him cough. After carrying the kitten a long way he drops him in the bamboo grove and disappears."},{"name":"主人","reading":"しゅじん","category":"character","keys":["主人","主人は","彼"],"content":"The master of the house and a schoolteacher, who is unnamed in the passage. He rarely meets the narrator and shuts himself in his study after school, though the family thinks he is a great scholar, and he pretends to be one. In reality he is lazy: he naps, drools on his books, and has a weak stomach with a sallow, flabby complexion. He eats heavily, takes Takadiastase, and reads only a few pages before falling asleep each night. He talks little and twirls the black hair under his nose. He took in the narrator with the words 'そんなら内へ置いてやれ' and the narrator often sits on his lap while he reads the newspaper. He wakes easily at the children's crying and is nervous (神経胃弱性), and he complains to his visitors about how hard teaching is."},{"name":"下女","reading":"げじょ","category":"character","keys":["下女"],"content":"A maid in the household who works in the kitchen. She is hostile to the narrator, repeatedly chasing him away from the kitchen and the rice tub. She carried him by the scruff to the master, complaining that the stray kitten keeps coming into the kitchen, and she threw him out onto the kitchen floor with resentment after the master said he could stay."},{"name":"細君","reading":"さいくん","category":"character","keys":["細君"],"content":"The master's wife, who is unnamed in the passage. She was very angry when the narrator scratched the tatami while sharpening his claws, and afterwards does not easily let him into the parlour. She is part of the household that turns on the narrator, chasing him with the children."},{"name":"小供","reading":"こども","category":"character","keys":["小供","小さい方","子供"],"content":"The master's two children, aged five and three, who sleep together in one room at night. The narrator sneaks into their bed to sleep, and the younger one is especially nasty: when she wakes and sees him, she shrieks 'the cat came' and wakes the household. They treat the narrator cruelly, turning him upside down, putting a bag over his head, throwing him, and stuffing him into the stove, and the whole family joins in when he tries to retaliate."},{"name":"白君","reading":"しろくん","category":"character","keys":["白君"],"content":"A cat in the house opposite (筋向), whom the narrator respects. He is a neighbour who says at every meeting that humans are the most unkind of creatures. He recently gave birth to four kittens, but a 書生 in his household took them to the pond behind the house on the third day, and the passage breaks off while describing the drowning."}]}
